/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A spar is a spanwise beam running from root to tip.
   - The skin between the front and rear spar, together with the two webs, closes a cell called the torsion box.
   - wbox is the torsion box width in m; xfront and xrear are the front and rear spar positions as a fraction of chord (0 to 1); c is the chord in m.
   - Shear force and bending moment along the span are worked out in Shear force and bending moment distribution on wings; the shear flow inside this closed box comes later, with torsion of closed sections (the Bredt-Batho theory).
   - A rib is a chordwise member: it runs from the leading edge to the trailing edge, crossing the box.
   - A stringer is a small spanwise stiffener on the inside face of the skin, closely spaced.
   - The leading edge, ahead of the front spar, and the trailing edge, behind the rear spar, are lighter structures.
   - The fuselage is a semi-monocoque shell: skin, frames, longerons and bulkheads.
   - A fuselage is also a beam: the tail unit loads the rear fuselage in bending and torsion, and the wing loads the centre section.
   - Doors, windows, access panels and wheel wells cut skin out of the shell.
*/
