/* book Worked example: Q: An aircraft floor panel has two aluminium faces, each 0.5 mm thick with E = 70 GPa, bonded to a 20 mm honeycomb core. (a) Find the bending stiffness D per metre width. (b) A solid aluminium sheet of the same mass per unit area, with the core weight neglected, is 1.0 mm thick. Find its D and the ratio of the two stiffnesses.
   1. Convert to metres: tf = 0.5 mm = 0.5 × 10⁻³ m; core thickness = 20 mm = 0.020 m; Ef = 70 GPa = 70 × 10⁹ Pa.
   2. Distance between face mid-planes: d = 0.020 + 0.0005 = 0.0205 m.
   3. Square it: d² = 0.0205² = 4.2025 × 10⁻⁴ m².
   4. Compute Ef tf = 70 × 10⁹ × 0.5 × 10⁻³ = 3.5 × 10⁷ N/m.
   5. Multiply: 3.5 × 10⁷ × 4.2025 × 10⁻⁴ = 1.4709 × 10⁴ N·m.
   6. Halve it: D = 7.354 × 10³ N·m = 7.35 kN·m per metre width.
   7. Equal-mass solid sheet: 2 × 0.5 mm = 1.0 mm = 1.0 × 10⁻³ m. D = E t³/12 = 70 × 10⁹ × (1.0 × 10⁻³)³ / 12 = 5.83 N·m per metre width.
   8. Ratio = 7354 / 5.83 = 1261.
   Answer: The sandwich panel has D = 7.35 kN·m per metre width against 5.83 N·m for the equal-mass solid sheet, so the sandwich is about 1261 times stiffer in bending. */
