/* book exam traps: Quoting only the faces' own bending stiffness, 2 × Ef tf³/12, and forgetting the d² term. That d² term is what the whole idea is about. | Using the core thickness as d and forgetting to add one face thickness. With 20 mm core and 0.5 mm faces, d is 20.5 mm, not 20 mm. | Checking face stress only. Core shear and debonding are common critical modes, and a panel that passes the face check can still fail in service. | Treating honeycomb as isotropic. Stiffness and shear strength differ strongly between the ribbon direction and across it, so orientation must be specified. | Mixing units: putting tf in millimetres into a formula where E is in Pa and d is in metres. The answer comes out a thousand times wrong. */
